WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:33:43.600 --> 00:33:47.099
So welcome. Today we're talking with Ian Gibbons, who nearly

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:51.132
50 years ago discovered the molecular protein, dynein.

00:33:51.133 --> 00:33:56.299
So dynein is a force generating ATPase that causes cilia and

00:33:56.300 --> 00:34:01.566
flagella to beat. It's also found in the cytoplasm of most eukaryotic

00:34:01.567 --> 00:34:05.432
cells, where it transports all kinds of different cargo, such as small

00:34:05.433 --> 00:34:10.132
membrane vesicles, the golgi apparatus, the nucleus, it transports

00:34:10.133 --> 00:34:15.032
mRNAs and protein complexes. And in mitosis it also plays an important

00:34:15.033 --> 00:34:19.966
role in constructing the mitotic spindle, so it's a very important molecule

00:34:19.967 --> 00:34:29.099
in cell biology. And well, I've known Ian for many years. We've collaborated

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:33.399
on several occasions. But I've never had a chance really to sit down and

00:34:33.400 --> 00:34:37.066
talk to you about how this whole story came about, how you

00:34:37.067 --> 00:34:43.332
went about discovering dynein. So, well, let's go back to when it all

00:34:43.333 --> 00:34:48.766
started in 1963, and maybe you can tell me about what captivated

00:34:48.767 --> 00:34:52.332
your interest in studying cilia and flagella in the first place.

00:34:52.333 --> 00:34:56.699
I was working as an electron microscopist at Harvard, in the biology

00:34:56.700 --> 00:35:02.766
labs. And I was collaborating on a project that involved looking at

00:35:02.767 --> 00:35:10.266
flagella in various flagellated protozoa. And one of the days, we

00:35:10.267 --> 00:35:16.532
happened to take some pictures by accident of the set of cross-sections

00:35:16.533 --> 00:35:20.266
of cilia and flagella and the transition between the basal body or centriole

00:35:20.267 --> 00:35:26.599
up into the shaft of the actinin. And there were enormous variety of

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:31.499
fibrillar structures there that had never been described previously.

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:36.332
And I felt that they must be doing something important. I knew that

00:35:36.333 --> 00:35:41.332
cilia and flagella were motile organisms, my supervisor at Cambridge

00:35:41.333 --> 00:35:45.732
where I was getting my PhD was John Bradfield, and he had actually written a paper

00:35:45.733 --> 00:35:49.832
on cilia and flagella mechanisms at that time. But then he left the field

00:35:49.833 --> 00:35:55.432
completely and went into finance. So I decided that when I took

00:35:55.433 --> 00:36:02.132
these beautiful structures, I really wanted to know how they might work.

00:36:02.133 --> 00:36:06.499
There was so much structure there that it was far from obvious as to

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:10.132
what it might do, how the different bits might interact.

00:36:10.133 --> 00:36:13.866
So which of these structures did you think might be the force

00:36:13.867 --> 00:36:18.766
generating entity? And how did you actually go about figuring that out?

00:36:18.767 --> 00:36:22.532
Well I didn't get there all in one go, it took several more times. Several

00:36:22.533 --> 00:36:29.266
stages. And it one of the things was that after I'd spent couple of years doing

00:36:29.267 --> 00:36:35.732
electron microscopy, I realized that one only got a rather limited distance

00:36:35.733 --> 00:36:38.799
looking at structure alone because there was almost nothing known

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:42.766
about the chemistry. And not a great deal about the detailed motility.

00:36:42.767 --> 00:36:51.232
And so, I decided to take on the biochemistry. Find out for myself that I had

00:36:51.233 --> 00:36:55.466
no experience in biochemistry, but I did have my wife, who was an experienced

00:36:55.467 --> 00:36:59.799
protein biochemist working in John Edsall's lab at the time.

00:36:59.800 --> 00:37:02.866
She certainly provided me with lots of help in getting me started

00:37:02.867 --> 00:37:06.632
and knowing the important questions that should be considered.

00:37:06.633 --> 00:37:13.432
So once I did that, then I decided that the Tetrahymena cilia were

00:37:13.433 --> 00:37:18.399
interested model organisms to isolate cilia, since one can grow them up

00:37:18.400 --> 00:37:24.199
by the pound and isolate them. Actually, I bought myself a cream separator

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:31.932
to harvest them. We did maybe about a pound in Tetrahymena in each culture.

00:37:31.933 --> 00:37:39.366
So then from that, I used the method for isolating the cilia that had been

00:37:39.367 --> 00:37:44.199
developed by Sir John Randall's lab in London, and used that to isolate the cilia.

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:49.932
His people had tried to look at the proteins, but they hadn't gotten very

00:37:49.933 --> 00:37:55.732
far, because they found that the proteins appeared to be too almost completely

00:37:55.733 --> 00:38:02.899
insoluble. I suspected the problem had been that the isolated

00:38:02.900 --> 00:38:06.299
cilia after they fractured from the cell, the cilia membrane healed

00:38:06.300 --> 00:38:11.966
over the isolated tip. And so the cilia was completely enveloped by

00:38:11.967 --> 00:38:15.099
the membrane, so the proteins couldn't get out. When I got these preparations

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:22.932
of isolated cilia, I decided that first thing to do get rid of the membrane.

00:38:22.933 --> 00:38:27.766
So I applied a mild detergent, digitonin, that did a wonderful job

00:38:27.767 --> 00:38:31.599
with taking off the membranes, and also showed that the structure

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:38.532
remained intact. All the internal bits of ciliary apparatus remained intact.

00:38:38.533 --> 00:38:46.466
And very importantly also, the cilia retained their axoneme and ATPase activity.

00:38:46.467 --> 00:38:50.432
Very little disappeared with the membrane, so we had the nuded

00:38:50.433 --> 00:38:57.566
cilia. I just put them into a concentrated solution of salt, and they completely dissolved.

00:38:57.567 --> 00:39:02.632
So you had access to all the protein, but it wasn't what I wanted.

00:39:02.633 --> 00:39:08.599
I wanted to get some fractionation, and that I achieved by extracting

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:13.132
with low ionic strength with EDTA, to completely remove any divalent

00:39:13.133 --> 00:39:18.699
cations. When I did that, I found I got a very interesting separation

00:39:18.700 --> 00:39:25.399
of the protein into two fractions, a soluble fraction and the residual

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:29.466
pellet. And comparing those two structures in the electron microscope,

00:39:29.467 --> 00:39:35.666
I saw that the extraction with low salt EDTA had removed all this central

00:39:35.667 --> 00:39:40.566
apparatus, the spokes, the two central tubules, and in addition,

00:39:40.567 --> 00:39:46.332
had removed the arms that ran around the length of the doublet

00:39:46.333 --> 00:39:53.066
microtubules. When I looked at the solubility of protein and ATPase,

00:39:53.067 --> 00:39:58.899
I found that this had indeed achieved a good separation, because

00:39:58.900 --> 00:40:10.066
the soluble fraction contained about 80% of the total initial ATPase

00:40:10.067 --> 00:40:14.999
activity. But only had about 30% of the total protein, so I'd obtained

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:18.466
something at a 3-fold purification just by doing this simple extraction.

00:40:18.467 --> 00:40:24.099
And so, since I had most of the axoneme model and their ATPase

00:40:24.100 --> 00:40:27.532
present in the soluble fraction, the obvious thing to do was to look at

00:40:27.533 --> 00:40:31.132
it. And the only technique that one had at the time, and this was before

00:40:31.133 --> 00:40:35.299
gel electrophoresis, the only technique available was centrifugation.

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:40.866
Started with an analytical centrifuge, and there were three peaks.

00:40:40.867 --> 00:40:46.399
One slow one at 4S, one at 14S, and one at 30S. When I ran

00:40:46.400 --> 00:40:50.566
this same material on a sucrose density gradient and assayed for

00:40:50.567 --> 00:40:55.366
ATPase activity, then I found that the ATPase activity was completely

00:40:55.367 --> 00:41:01.266
confined to the 14S and 30S fractions, the 4S had gone essentially null.

00:41:01.267 --> 00:41:06.932
Though it was the largest fraction in total protein. So then the question

00:41:06.933 --> 00:41:10.932
was, which of these various structures that had disappeared in the

00:41:10.933 --> 00:41:15.999
axoneme did the ATPase actually come from? And to find that out,

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:21.566
I tried recombining. I took the soluble fraction and the pellet fraction and

00:41:21.567 --> 00:41:26.232
mixed them together, in the presence of magnesium. And fortunately,

00:41:26.233 --> 00:41:31.666
I found that a major fraction of the ATPase activity that had been

00:41:31.667 --> 00:41:38.632
extracted in solution became rebound to the pellet fraction. Looking in the

00:41:38.633 --> 00:41:43.632
electron microscope, this showed that the structures that had reappeared

00:41:43.633 --> 00:41:47.532
when the ATPase activity became rebound were the two arms that

00:41:47.533 --> 00:41:51.166
stick out from each of the doublet tubules. So this was pretty

00:41:51.167 --> 00:41:55.232
strong evidence that the axonemal ATPase was indeed

00:41:55.233 --> 00:41:59.966
localized in these arms, around the doublet tubules.

00:41:59.967 --> 00:42:03.899
So now you had this ATPase, you could show that you could add it back

00:42:03.900 --> 00:42:07.166
and it could reconstitute this really interesting structure between

00:42:07.167 --> 00:42:12.766
the two outer doublets. So next step, how did you come up with the

00:42:12.767 --> 00:42:15.166
name of this interesting ATPase?

00:42:15.167 --> 00:42:18.432
Once we had the protein more purified, we gave it the name dynein.

00:42:18.433 --> 00:42:23.666
Dynein means, "dyne" was the unit of force, the only unit of force present

00:42:23.667 --> 00:42:26.766
when I went through school. Actually, Barbara was the one who

00:42:26.767 --> 00:42:30.366
suggested the name dynein, she suggested it one evening at home.

00:42:30.367 --> 00:42:33.132
So at that time, how did you think it worked?

00:42:33.133 --> 00:42:38.099
Well, there were two theories running. And there were two possible

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:41.699
mechanisms. One would be a mechanism involving localized

00:42:41.700 --> 00:42:48.466
contractions, that somehow the localized sections on the length

00:42:48.467 --> 00:42:53.332
of the microtubule might change the crystallized packing arrangement

00:42:53.333 --> 00:42:58.099
of subunits, in a way similar to that of the bacterial flagellum when it

00:42:58.100 --> 00:43:01.032
changes its shape from a left handed helix to a right handed helix

00:43:01.033 --> 00:43:05.966
under the influence of a chemotactic attractant. That will be one

00:43:05.967 --> 00:43:11.399
possible mechanism. Second possible mechanism was that microtubules would slide

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:16.366
relative to one another, and when this opposed by some resistance to bending,

00:43:16.367 --> 00:43:21.266
would cause a formation of a new bend. The difficulty with that mechanism

00:43:21.267 --> 00:43:25.899
was that to have bends of opposite directions at different positions

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:31.132
along the length of the axoneme meant that the individual microtubules

00:43:31.133 --> 00:43:34.166
-- the microtubules run straight along the length of the axoneme,

00:43:34.167 --> 00:43:38.766
but it meant that sliding has to be in opposite directions in order to

00:43:38.767 --> 00:43:43.432
generate bends of opposite directions at different positions.

00:43:43.433 --> 00:43:48.766
Even where you're only thinking of a single microtubule.

00:43:48.767 --> 00:43:58.166
That seemed to be a major obstacle. And so, I didn't take it any further.

00:43:58.167 --> 00:44:01.466
At that time, I actually looked at the text of the paper and was actually

00:44:01.467 --> 00:44:04.466
leaning toward a contractile hypothesis.

00:44:04.467 --> 00:44:08.966
So then what did you do next? It was time to do an experiment

00:44:08.967 --> 00:44:13.266
and so what kind of experiment to discern between these two mechanisms?

00:44:13.267 --> 00:44:16.832
After we'd taken the formality of publishing the more purified protein,

00:44:16.833 --> 00:44:24.066
and the name, which was in 1965, then I realized that we needed to study

00:44:24.067 --> 00:44:27.999
yet more into the motility. And the Tetrahymena cilia were extremely

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:31.899
inconvenient for doing that, because they're only 5um long and

00:44:31.900 --> 00:44:37.266
though you can see them wagging if you use a high power light

00:44:37.267 --> 00:44:43.166
microscope lens, but you could never measure accurately the parameters

00:44:43.167 --> 00:44:47.932
of waveforms. So that was when I decided that we should shift to

00:44:47.933 --> 00:44:51.866
sea urchin sperm flagella. Because these are about 40um long.

00:44:51.867 --> 00:44:56.099
About 8 times as long as the Tetrahymena cilia. Their motility

00:44:56.100 --> 00:45:00.166
had already been studied by Sir James Gray, who'd shown that

00:45:00.167 --> 00:45:03.799
they propagate waves along the length of the flagellum.

00:45:03.800 --> 00:45:07.732
But we didn't have a really good source of them in Cambridge, Massachusetts,

00:45:07.733 --> 00:45:13.332
where I was then. And so we waited and an opportunity came up

00:45:13.333 --> 00:45:18.999
for me to move to Hawaii, the University of Hawaii. And I decided that

00:45:19.000 --> 00:45:23.166
would be an opportunity to switch completely to using sea urchin sperm

00:45:23.167 --> 00:45:28.799
since the sea urchins in Hawaii were sexually ripe all year round.

00:45:28.800 --> 00:45:32.599
You would have material for working with them year round, without having

00:45:32.600 --> 00:45:36.666
the complications that would happen if you tried to do something like

00:45:36.667 --> 00:45:41.299
California or on the East Coast. So we moved to Hawaii and then we started

00:45:41.300 --> 00:45:46.299
studying the motility in more detail. We first measured the parameters of

00:45:46.300 --> 00:45:50.299
the bending waves of the live sperm, and then we took membranes

00:45:50.300 --> 00:45:52.632
off the way we had been doing for the Tetrahymena cilia.

00:45:52.633 --> 00:45:56.532
Showed that they could be reactivated with ATP and propagated

00:45:56.533 --> 00:46:02.399
bends very nicely. Then the question was the mechanical force actually

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:10.799
going on in the axoneme? And Keith Summers, who had

00:46:10.800 --> 00:46:14.999
-- my graduate student at the time, had the idea of using

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:18.899
trypsin to weaken the structure, he thought that might do

00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:22.832
something. I had earlier observed that with the Tetrahymena cilia,

00:46:22.833 --> 00:46:26.599
they become swollen when you gave them ATP, they didn't slide.

00:46:26.600 --> 00:46:32.266
But they became swollen. So he tried to make this happen also with the

00:46:32.267 --> 00:46:36.632
sea urchin sperm flagella, but was completely unsuccessful. They didn't

00:46:36.633 --> 00:46:41.132
do the same trick. So he treated them with a little bit of trypsin to see

00:46:41.133 --> 00:46:44.966
if that would get them to do the same trick, and well it did, but

00:46:44.967 --> 00:46:48.232
it also did something much more dramatic because it just seemed to

00:46:48.233 --> 00:46:51.666
disappear completely. If you took the demembranated isolated

00:46:51.667 --> 00:46:55.832
axonemes, treated them weekly with trypsin, and then diffused in

00:46:55.833 --> 00:47:01.132
ATP slowly from the edge of the microscope slide, as the front of ATP

00:47:01.133 --> 00:47:04.232
reached the axonemes, it just seemed to fade and disappear.

00:47:04.233 --> 00:47:09.432
So something quite dramatic was going on, something that

00:47:09.433 --> 00:47:10.966
did not happen with the Tetrahymena cilia.

00:47:10.967 --> 00:47:14.366
And you wanted to look at that directly and observe it.

00:47:14.367 --> 00:47:17.699
I decided to use light and dark field microscopy to watch

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:24.166
the disappearing of these flagellar axonemes. So Barbara and I

00:47:24.167 --> 00:47:28.832
went back one evening and we looked and we saw it, and we were

00:47:28.833 --> 00:47:33.699
amazed that they really split apart. It was really dramatic.

00:47:33.700 --> 00:47:38.432
And that was really one of the high points, because it was obviously

00:47:38.433 --> 00:47:45.099
demonstrating a very fundamental mechanism between

00:47:45.100 --> 00:47:49.499
the interaction of a motor protein and microtubule for the first time.

00:47:49.500 --> 00:47:54.532
And it was happening under our own eyes. One of the things we

00:47:54.533 --> 00:47:57.699
did was still to get more evidence that dynein was actually

00:47:57.700 --> 00:48:02.399
the motor driving the motility. The way we did that was by

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:08.299
partially extracting the dynein arms, when we did that, we found

00:48:08.300 --> 00:48:12.666
that the beat frequency decreased proportionally to the

00:48:12.667 --> 00:48:16.466
fraction of dynein arms that had been removed. And then

00:48:16.467 --> 00:48:20.232
we could add the solubilized dynein back again and the beat

00:48:20.233 --> 00:48:23.766
flagella beat frequency increased almost back to its original

00:48:23.767 --> 00:48:27.199
level as the arms became rebound. That was really the

00:48:27.200 --> 00:48:33.399
confirming evidence, the last nail in the coffin, or whatever you want to call it.

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:38.366
So we've covered the isolation and discovery of the ATPase, dynein,

00:48:38.367 --> 00:48:46.099
to the in vitro demonstration that dynein can slide microtubules, and that

00:48:46.100 --> 00:48:51.799
was the underlying principle mechanism that gave rise to ciliar

00:48:51.800 --> 00:48:55.266
and flagellar beating. So maybe you can give us a quick tour

00:48:55.267 --> 00:48:57.932
of dynein after that.

00:48:57.933 --> 00:49:00.399
So at that point, we wanted to characterize it biochemically.

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:06.399
Which we did, and by that time, membrane gels, gel electrophoresis

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:09.666
had been discovered and developed. So we were able to start looking

00:49:09.667 --> 00:49:12.632
at the polypeptides that were present in our preparations.

00:49:12.633 --> 00:49:15.766
Something that had not been possible when we did the original

00:49:15.767 --> 00:49:19.466
extraction of the dynein, because no gel was available

00:49:19.467 --> 00:49:25.066
that were appropriate at that time. So that was one of the things, we

00:49:25.067 --> 00:49:29.299
worked up and purified the ATPase and started looking at its

00:49:29.300 --> 00:49:35.099
polypeptides. We eventually cloned and sequenced one of the polypeptides

00:49:35.100 --> 00:49:41.566
from the outer arm, and then after we cloned and sequenced it, we

00:49:41.567 --> 00:49:50.032
started looking at the different forms of it. Because it turned out,

00:49:50.033 --> 00:49:53.799
as most people discovered with gel electrophoresis, preparations were

00:49:53.800 --> 00:49:57.066
always more complicated than you'd like to think. And a lot more

00:49:57.067 --> 00:50:00.999
proteins were present than one really liked to think. So we discovered

00:50:01.000 --> 00:50:04.832
there were about 8 or 9 bands in the electrophoretic

00:50:04.833 --> 00:50:09.532
position of the dynein heavy chains. So we isolated different

00:50:09.533 --> 00:50:12.999
ones of them, studied how many there were, and that led to

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:19.866
realizing that the dynein family of the heavy chains alone consists

00:50:19.867 --> 00:50:26.032
of at least 9 subfamilies. And two of these correspond to the two

00:50:26.033 --> 00:50:31.666
cytoplasmic dyneins, the other seven are axonemal proteins, and

00:50:31.667 --> 00:50:35.132
sometimes there's more than one represented of a family present at one

00:50:35.133 --> 00:50:39.332
time. So the usual flagellar axonemes that we were using contained

00:50:39.333 --> 00:50:46.132
13 actual gene products. So, we'd cloned and sequenced one of these

00:50:46.133 --> 00:50:49.832
genes. The heavy chain, the beta heavy chain, the outer arm dynein.

00:50:49.833 --> 00:50:55.566
Cloned little bits of others. But of course as time went by, other people joined in

00:50:55.567 --> 00:51:00.699
and so by now, there's a really elaborate tree showing the nine

00:51:00.700 --> 00:51:06.899
subfamilies of the dynein heavy chain form independent subfamilies

00:51:06.900 --> 00:51:11.266
that were present even in the last common ancestor of all eukaryotes.

00:51:11.267 --> 00:51:18.932
In 1997, we wanted to retire from the University of Hawaii, less than full time.

00:51:18.933 --> 00:51:26.266
So we moved to California to be closer to our family, our children, and

00:51:26.267 --> 00:51:33.199
there I started working on the cytoplasmic microtubule binding domain

00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:39.032
of cytoplasmic dynein. And by taking just that separate small piece

00:51:39.033 --> 00:51:43.932
of just microtubule binding domain with just its coiled coiled stalk, which is only about

00:51:43.933 --> 00:51:49.466
5% of the total heavy chain of the mass of the whole dynein heavy chain.

00:51:49.467 --> 00:51:53.599
And we were able to express it in bacteria, study its properties.

00:51:53.600 --> 00:51:57.499
We were able to show that by changing the register between

00:51:57.500 --> 00:52:00.432
the two strands of the coiled-coiled stalk, we could modulate

00:52:00.433 --> 00:52:09.299
the affinity constant with which this expressed microtubule

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:14.299
binding domain would bind to native cytoplasmic microtubules.

00:52:14.300 --> 00:52:20.866
Having got there and having this expressed functional cytoplasmic

00:52:20.867 --> 00:52:25.599
microtubule binding domain, the next step obviously was to

00:52:25.600 --> 00:52:29.599
go on and crystallize it and get a structure. Took a year,

00:52:29.600 --> 00:52:34.332
but Joan Garbarino in my lab did a wonderful job and

00:52:34.333 --> 00:52:39.432
then helped especially with the crystallography, we joined

00:52:39.433 --> 00:52:44.366
Andrew Carter from your own lab and together, we managed to get

00:52:44.367 --> 00:52:50.899
a reasonable atomic level structure for microtubule binding.

00:52:50.900 --> 00:52:55.666
Are there any particular thoughts you have? Just looking back over this?

00:52:55.667 --> 00:53:00.132
My general modus of operandi, I think has been to work with a

00:53:00.133 --> 00:53:03.566
technique for a while, and if it seems to be sticking, try another

00:53:03.567 --> 00:53:06.899
side -- try climbing another side of the mountain. Change your

00:53:06.900 --> 00:53:09.899
technique and start a fraction where you approach it in a completely

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:15.632
different way. And I think that's useful, I definitely found it interesting.

00:53:15.633 --> 00:53:18.466
I don't know how well it would work today, where there are many more

00:53:18.467 --> 00:53:21.232
people in science. And most top problems are more popular,

00:53:21.233 --> 00:53:24.632
competition might make it difficult for you to do that. But certainly, it

00:53:24.633 --> 00:53:27.299
worked very well for me, so I would encourage anyone else

00:53:27.300 --> 00:53:32.199
